Status: NORMAL
The operating system manages hardware resources and coordinates background worker threads. Mutual exclusion ensures that shared resources are accessed safely by at most one thread at a time. A thread pool reduces the overhead of frequent thread creation and destruction. Producer-consumer queues decouple task generation from task execution in concurrent systems. Amdahl's law defines the theoretical maximum speedup achievable through parallel processing. Race conditions occur when the output depends on the non-deterministic scheduling of concurrent threads. Atomic operations and condition variables enable low-latency synchronization without busy waiting. ThreadShield guarantees isolated analysis within the dedicated workspace environment.

